  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA line of showers and thunderstorms will continue to travel \nsoutheast across the forecast area early this morning. Additional \nrounds of rounds of showers and storms are then expected to traverse \nthe area as a cold front slowly approaches during the day, and then \ndescends through the area this evening into early Friday morning.\n\nSevere potential for today should be somewhat limited as cloud cover \nand ongoing activity help to temper instability. That said, can't \ncompletely rule out a few stronger storms capable of producing \ngusty winds. \n\nFlash flooding will remain the primary concern for this event, \nprimarily due to excessive rainfall wherever training storms \ndevelop. Some instances of flash flooding and rises on small creeks \nand streams have already been observed as a result of rapid \naccumulations in training storms over parts of southeast Ohio this \nmorning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWhile activity initially diminishes behind a cold front Friday \nmorning, moisture is likely to linger over the area as the boundary \nstalls near the VA/KY/NC/TN border. Residual moisture combined with \ndaytime heating may facilitate development of showers and storms \nacross the southeast half of the area Friday afternoon and evening.\nUnsettled weather then persists as the aforementioned boundary \nlifts north as a warm front this weekend. \n\nLocalized flash flooding could be possible into the weekend if \nheavy downpours occur over areas that were impacted by heavy \nrain earlier in the week.\n\nA brief reprieve may be possible at the beginning of the work \nweek, then moisture and precipitation chances are expected to \nincrease as a system propels yet another front towards the area\nmid to late week.",
